repository-code: 'https://github.com/WangLabCSU/SigBridgeR'
url: 'https://wanglabcsu.github.io/SigBridgeR/'
abstract: >-
  Background

  Single-cell RNA sequencing (scRNA-seq) has revolutionized
  our understanding of cellular heterogeneity, yet linking
  specific cell subpopulations to clinically relevant
  phenotypes remains a major challenge. Although multiple
  computational methods have been developed to integrate
  bulk transcriptomic and single-cell data for
  phenotype-associated cell identification, differences in
  algorithmic strategies, parameter configurations, software
  implementations, and output formats make it difficult to
  compare methods fairly, reproduce analyses, and determine
  which approach is most suitable for a given biological
  question.


  Results

  Here, we present SigBridgeR, an extensible R framework
  that integrates eight representative phenotype-associated
  cell subpopulation identification algorithms within a
  unified workflow. Using paired bulk RNA-seq and scRNA-seq
  datasets from four cancer types under both binary and
  survival phenotypes, we performed a comprehensive
  benchmark combining positive and negative control
  experiments, accuracy evaluation, Hallmark pathway
  enrichment analysis, parameter sensitivity analysis, and
  computational performance profiling. The benchmark
  revealed substantial differences in algorithm robustness,
  biological consistency, computational efficiency, and
  parameter sensitivity. Across multiple evaluation
  criteria, Scissor and scAB consistently achieved the most
  balanced overall performance, whereas several methods
  exhibited important limitations, including poor
  scalability, high parameter sensitivity, or unstable
  phenotype detection.


  Conclusions

  SigBridgeR provides standardized preprocessing, unified
  algorithm interfaces, and an extensible registry-based
  architecture for phenotype-associated cell subpopulation
  analysis. By integrating systematic benchmarking and
  optimized implementations into a single platform,
  SigBridgeR facilitates reliable evaluation of existing
  methods, simplifies multi-algorithm analyses, and supports
  the development of improved phenotype-guided single-cell
  analysis methods for precision medicine.
keywords:
  - single-cell RNA sequencing
  - phenotype-associated subpopulation
  - bulk transcriptomics
  - computational benchmarking
  - R package
  - precision medicine
